# NOTE: every credential below is a placeholder. Put your own values in .env, which is gitignored --
# never here. A real password in a committed example is published the moment the repository is, and
# it stays in the history after it is edited out.
#
# Local development environment. Copy to .env:
#
#   make env
#
# The Makefile loads .env and exports every name in it, so nothing has to be typed and no command is
# shell-specific. .env is read by make, not by the binary: the service still takes its configuration
# from the process environment alone, and internal/config refuses a wrong value by name at startup.
#
# Values carry no surrounding quotes. make passes the rest of the line verbatim, so a quoted DSN
# arrives with its quotes attached and fails as a connection error.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What the test suite needs: enough for `make test`, `make test-integration` and `make test-ci`,
# with no Keycloak.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The administrative connection. Integration tests create and drop schema, so this is the owner
# rather than a runtime role. internal/controldb then connects as the runtime role to prove it holds
# no ownership and no DDL.
TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgres://postgres:CHANGEME@localhost:5432/identity_control_dev?sslmode=disable

# Used by `make migrate`, `make migrate-status` and the migration stages.
IDENTITY_MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL=postgres://postgres:CHANGEME@localhost:5432/identity_control_dev?sslmode=disable

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What `make run` needs in addition. Without a Keycloak, `make test-ci` is how this service is
# checked locally.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The runtime login role, which inherits identity_runtime and owns nothing. Deliberately not the
# administrative password: running the service as the owner would make every ownership assertion
# pass for the wrong reason.
IDENTITY_DATABASE_URL=postgres://identity_app:CHANGEME@localhost:5432/identity_control_dev?sslmode=disable

# Keycloak. The clients authenticate with a key pair, never a secret (STD-IAM-001 §3): each setting
# is a path to a private key file, which deploy/dev/create-kernel-clients.sh and
# create-registration-client.sh make. Key files are gitignored (*.pem, deploy/dev/keys/).
IDENTITY_KEYCLOAK_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8081
IDENTITY_KEYCLOAK_REALM=scnehaux
IDENTITY_KEYCLOAK_CLIENT_ID=identity-control
IDENTITY_KEYCLOAK_CLIENT_KEY_FILE=deploy/dev/keys/identity-control.pem
IDENTITY_REGISTRATION_KEYCLOAK_CLIENT_ID=identity-control-registration
IDENTITY_REGISTRATION_KEYCLOAK_CLIENT_KEY_FILE=deploy/dev/keys/identity-control-registration.pem

# Compared for exact equality, so a trailing slash refuses every token and reads as a permissions bug.
IDENTITY_TOKEN_ISSUER=http://127.0.0.1:8081/realms/scnehaux
IDENTITY_TOKEN_AUDIENCE=identity-control
IDENTITY_JWKS_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8081/realms/scnehaux/protocol/openid-connect/certs

IDENTITY_LISTEN_ADDRESS=127.0.0.1:8097

# Production unless stated, so a deployment that forgets to say keeps production's rules. A local
# run says otherwise: a registration then needs one owner rather than two.
IDENTITY_ENVIRONMENT=non-production

# Organization Control's workload principal_id: the one caller the provider authority intake,
# POST /v1/deliveries, admits (TDD-identity-control-006). Unset, the intake answers 503.
# IDENTITY_DELIVERY_PRINCIPAL_ID=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000

# Organization Control, which this service reads as the provider authority consumer: the snapshot
# `make provider-bootstrap` builds the projection from, the frontier the freshness poll reads, and
# progress (TDD-identity-control-006). Unset, nothing is read: the projection is never fresh and no
# activation is honored. Once the base URL is set, the workload client below is required. The key
# is a path to a private key file, never a secret (STD-IAM-001 §3); key files are gitignored.
# IDENTITY_ORGANIZATION_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8099
# IDENTITY_WORKLOAD_CLIENT_ID=identity-control-workload
# IDENTITY_WORKLOAD_KEY_FILE=deploy/dev/keys/identity-control-workload.pem
# IDENTITY_WORKLOAD_TOKEN_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8081/realms/scnehaux/protocol/openid-connect/token
# The kernel issuer the assertion names (RFC 7523 §3): IDENTITY_TOKEN_ISSUER's value.
# IDENTITY_WORKLOAD_AUDIENCE=http://127.0.0.1:8081/realms/scnehaux
# IDENTITY_PROVIDER_FRESHNESS=60s

# Optional. Defaults live in internal/config/config.go; listed so the knobs are discoverable.
# LOG_LEVEL=info
# IDENTITY_TOKEN_TYPE=report
# IDENTITY_TOKEN_MAX_SKEW=30s
# IDENTITY_PROVISION_TIMEOUT=10s
# IDENTITY_PENDING_RECOVERY_AFTER=60s
# IDENTITY_RECONCILE_PAGE_SIZE=200
# IDENTITY_REGISTRATION_RECONCILE_INTERVAL=1h
# IDENTITY_CLIENT_KEY_LIFETIME=2160h
# IDENTITY_CLIENT_KEY_ROTATION_OVERLAP=168h
# IDENTITY_UNMANAGED_CLIENTS=report
# DB_MAX_CONNS=20
# DB_MAX_CONN_LIFETIME=30m
# DB_ACQUIRE_TIMEOUT=3s
# HTTP_READ_TIMEOUT=10s
# HTTP_WRITE_TIMEOUT=30s
# HTTP_REQUEST_TIMEOUT=5s
# HTTP_MAX_IN_FLIGHT=256
# HTTP_SHUTDOWN_GRACE=20s

# Keeps the Go build cache off a full C: drive. Adjust or remove for your machine.
# GOTMPDIR=D:\.gotmp
